  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The parabola's vertex lies on the symmetry axis, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-4$ and $b=-20$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-20)}{2(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-20)}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{5}{2}.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4{x}^{2}-20x-15 \\\\  & =-4{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-20(-\\frac{5}{2})-15 \\\\  & =-25+50-15 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-\\frac{5}{2},10).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
